
Gave an old 2d inverse kinematics python function to Copilot for porting to C++. Several changes were suggested. A check was added to make sure target radius was not 0. A clamp function replaced a manual check that the arccos function domain was not exceeded. A law-of-cosines calculation replaced the previous trigonometric solution for angle calculation. An option was added to the function to return an error if the target exceeded the arm / leg length, or set the second bone to an angle of 0. Previously the function forced the limb to straighten without returning an error. A conversion from degrees to radians and back to degrees was added according to my preference to use degrees at the user level. The new changes were then ported to a C++ script to use for a character in OpenGL.

Following that a function was added to read a loop of points that the 2d ik function could be set to depending a time modulus.

After that I began to develop a path system with Copilot. A function was added for reading an OBJ file containing a path mesh. Copilot added a function for changing the "up" axis to z if defined as y in the OBJ file. Functions were created to calculate vertex normals from vertex lists. Functions to create planes that lie along the vertex normals were added. This was so that a character could be tracked by checking if they were inside the four planes assigned to each triangle, one for the triangle face and 3 for each of the edges. Vertex normals were used so that characters or in-game robots could move along surfaces that are not restricted to the xy plane, and so that objects flying above the path could be tracked by the same system. A solution was worked out for preventing characters from sinking into walls or other obstacles that meet the path edge by offsetting the line from the border edge where the character should "collide". Functions for allowing the character to slide on the path edge at an angle were considered. A system for optimizing the lookup of path triangle start and destination points was added by Copilot. 
